What Jorge and others pointed out was an apparent inconsistency
in the refgrammar. That this inconsistency was due to sumti-raising
errors was only one possible way of resolving the problem. In
the way I read the debate, I read you as denying that there was
any inconsistency - i.e. as claiming that all relevant bits of
the refgram are pukka. Therefore, as Chris suggested, it seemed
that the only option was that ni/jei were homonymous. Only then
did the homonymy solution get propounded.

Also, calling the erroneous usage of ni/jei "sumti-raising" is
unhelpful, since using "tu`a le ni/jei" still does not fully
capture the intended meaning. Others called it "indirect question"
which is far clearer.

